% =============================================================================
% KEY GAPS IN PRIOR WORK (for Introduction section)
% =============================================================================
%
% GAP 1: NO PROPAGATION-AWARE METRIC FOR AGENT HALLUCINATIONS
%   Existing hallucination benchmarks (TruthfulQA, HaluEval, FActScore,
%   HalluLens) measure hallucination at a single generation step. Agent
%   benchmarks (tau-bench, AgentBench, WebArena) measure task-level
%   success/failure. No prior work quantifies how a hallucination at step k
%   propagates through subsequent tool calls to corrupt the final outcome
%   -- our Error Propagation Score (EPS) fills this gap.
%
% GAP 2: NO STEP-LEVEL HALLUCINATION ANNOTATION IN AGENTIC TRACES
%   While RAGTruth provides word-level hallucination annotation for single
%   RAG responses, and tau-bench tracks task-level pass/fail, there is no
%   existing dataset with per-step hallucination labels across multi-step
%   tool-use agent trajectories. Our benchmark provides the first such
%   annotated corpus.
%
% GAP 3: SELF-CONSISTENCY HAS NOT BEEN APPLIED TO TOOL-CALL VALIDATION
%   Self-consistency (Wang et al., 2023) and its extensions (SAC3, Active
%   Prompting) have been studied for reasoning tasks but never applied to
%   detect hallucinated tool calls (wrong function names, fabricated
%   arguments, phantom APIs). Our work is the first to use multi-sample
%   consistency checking for tool-call hallucination interception.
%
% GAP 4: LLM-JUDGE ENSEMBLES LACK RIGOROUS IAA REPORTING FOR BINARY
%   HALLUCINATION CLASSIFICATION
%   The LLM-as-judge literature (Zheng et al., G-Eval, Prometheus) focuses
%   on preference ranking or Likert scoring. Studies using Cohen/Fleiss
%   kappa for binary hallucination detection in agentic contexts are
%   absent. Our 3-judge ensemble with rigorous kappa reporting establishes
%   reliability baselines for this task.
%
% GAP 5: NO UNIFIED FRAMEWORK CONNECTING DETECTION, PROPAGATION, AND
%   INTERCEPTION
%   Prior work treats hallucination detection (SelfCheckGPT, Lynx),
%   guardrails (NeMo Guardrails), and agent evaluation (tau-bench,
%   AgentBench) as separate problems. Our framework unifies these into a
%   single pipeline: detect hallucinations at each step, measure their
%   propagation through the trajectory, and intercept them before they
%   corrupt downstream tool calls.
%
% =============================================================================
